1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,701
The pitch version of a data flywheel sounds automatic: ship it, users generate data,

2
00:00:05,701 --> 00:00:10,798
the data makes it better, better draws more users, the wheel spins itself faster

3
00:00:10,798 --> 00:00:11,689
every quarter.

4
00:00:11,689 --> 00:00:16,658
Said like that, it sounds like something that happens to a product rather than

5
00:00:16,658 --> 00:00:18,378
something a team has to do.

6
00:00:18,378 --> 00:00:20,927
It isn't automatic anywhere in the loop.

7
00:00:20,927 --> 00:00:26,023
Every step, from a rejected suggestion to an actual fix shipped, is a deliberate

8
00:00:26,023 --> 00:00:27,743
action someone has to take.

9
00:00:27,743 --> 00:00:31,247
Skip the deliberate part and you don't have a flywheel.

10
00:00:31,247 --> 00:00:36,407
You have 9.2's instrumentation, correctly capturing a rejection event that nobody

11
00:00:36,407 --> 00:00:37,681
ever looks at again.

12
00:00:37,681 --> 00:00:43,032
Every section in this course has quietly built toward this moment without naming it.

13
00:00:43,032 --> 00:00:45,262
Section four gave you a golden set.

14
00:00:45,262 --> 00:00:50,358
Section eight's incident rule turned one specific failure into a permanent case.

15
00:00:50,358 --> 00:00:55,709
This lecture generalises both: the same discipline, running continuously on ordinary

16
00:00:55,709 --> 00:00:58,130
usage, not just triggered by a crisis.

17
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:03,506
Exactly one arrow on this diagram draws itself: usage produces a signal, because

18
00:01:03,506 --> 00:01:07,186
9.2's instrumentation fires whether or not anyone's watching.

19
00:01:07,186 --> 00:01:10,142
Every arrow after that needs a person to push it.

20
00:01:10,142 --> 00:01:15,088
Sampling for review needs someone to decide how much gets looked at and how often.

21
00:01:15,088 --> 00:01:20,035
Turning a reviewed case into a golden set entry or a prompt fix needs someone with

22
00:01:20,035 --> 00:01:22,870
the authority and the time to make that change.

23
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:27,757
Shipping the improvement needs the normal release process this course has assumed

24
00:01:27,757 --> 00:01:28,360
all along.

25
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:33,186
Call any one of those steps optional, and the loop stops exactly there, quietly,

26
00:01:33,186 --> 00:01:35,478
while the usage number keeps climbing.

27
00:01:35,478 --> 00:01:36,805
Who would even notice?

28
00:01:36,805 --> 00:01:41,752
Notice, too, what this diagram deliberately leaves out: a direct arrow from Signal

29
00:01:41,752 --> 00:01:43,502
straight to Improved feature.

30
00:01:43,502 --> 00:01:48,267
That shortcut is exactly the automated version this lecture is warning you away

31
00:01:48,267 --> 00:01:50,801
from, missing from the drawing on purpose.

32
00:01:50,801 --> 00:01:55,687
Every real version of this loop routes through a person at the review step, every

33
00:01:55,687 --> 00:01:56,894
time, no exceptions.

34
00:01:56,894 --> 00:02:01,840
Compare this loop against 4.2's golden set build process: the same shape stretched

35
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:03,952
out over time instead of done once.

36
00:02:03,952 --> 00:02:08,778
A golden set was fifty cases, chosen deliberately, scored against a rubric, at a

37
00:02:08,778 --> 00:02:10,467
single moment before launch.

38
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:15,293
This loop is the same deliberate selection and review, running in small batches,

39
00:02:15,293 --> 00:02:16,620
forever, after launch.

40
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:21,356
Notice the good column isn't describing a bigger engineering effort.

41
00:02:21,356 --> 00:02:26,404
It's describing the exact same habit 8.6 already asked for after an incident, just

42
00:02:26,404 --> 00:02:30,714
running on a calendar instead of waiting for something to break first.

43
00:02:30,714 --> 00:02:35,577
That's the actual insight worth taking from this lecture: a rejected or heavily

44
00:02:35,577 --> 00:02:40,564
edited output is a small incident, informative in exactly the same way, even when

45
00:02:40,564 --> 00:02:43,149
nothing broke badly enough to page anyone.

46
00:02:43,149 --> 00:02:47,951
Review it with the same seriousness, on a schedule, and the loop closes weekly

47
00:02:47,951 --> 00:02:50,783
instead of only after a crisis forces it open.

48
00:02:50,783 --> 00:02:55,893
Notice, too, how cheap the good column actually is compared to almost anything else

49
00:02:55,893 --> 00:02:58,047
this course has asked you to build.

50
00:02:58,047 --> 00:03:01,679
No new infrastructure, no new model, no new eval framework.

51
00:03:01,679 --> 00:03:06,666
Thirty minutes, one person, one calendar entry, and a habit of actually acting on

52
00:03:06,666 --> 00:03:10,360
what gets found in that half hour instead of filing it away.

53
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:15,285
Ask which of the five diagram steps your own feature is actually missing, rather

54
00:03:15,285 --> 00:03:18,609
than assuming the whole loop is either working or not.

55
00:03:18,609 --> 00:03:23,041
Most teams already have usage and signal working, since 9.2 covers both.

56
00:03:23,041 --> 00:03:28,213
The break is almost always at sampling or review, the two steps that need a person's

57
00:03:28,213 --> 00:03:30,737
calendar time rather than a line of code.

58
00:03:31,287 --> 00:03:36,396
Say the honest caveat, because "let the system learn from real usage automatically"

59
00:03:36,396 --> 00:03:41,444
sounds like the flywheel finishing itself, and it's actually a new, specific risk.

60
00:03:41,444 --> 00:03:46,369
Unfiltered public input feeding directly back into what the feature does next is

61
00:03:46,369 --> 00:03:51,170
exactly the shape 8.2 warned about: content from outside your control silently

62
00:03:51,170 --> 00:03:56,218
steering behaviour, just arriving through a training pipeline instead of a prompt.

63
00:03:56,218 --> 00:04:01,328
A reviewer between the signal and the fix isn't friction slowing the flywheel down.

64
00:04:01,328 --> 00:04:06,376
It's the component that stops the loop from learning the wrong lesson from a small

65
00:04:06,376 --> 00:04:09,269
number of bad-faith or simply unusual requests.

66
00:04:09,269 --> 00:04:14,317
This is also why the review step needs to be a person with judgement, not a second

67
00:04:14,317 --> 00:04:19,426
model quietly triaging the first model's mistakes with no human in the loop at all.

68
00:04:19,426 --> 00:04:24,474
A judge model reviewing rejected outputs can genuinely help at scale, the same way

69
00:04:24,474 --> 00:04:29,461
4.4 taught you to use one, but it still needs 4.4's calibration discipline behind

70
00:04:29,461 --> 00:04:34,016
it, not blind trust that a second model catches what the first one missed.

71
00:04:34,566 --> 00:04:39,413
That's the standard worth holding your own loop to, and the exercise is worth

72
00:04:39,413 --> 00:04:44,512
running exactly as written, on real rejected cases, not a hypothetical average of

73
00:04:44,512 --> 00:04:46,023
how review usually goes.

74
00:04:46,023 --> 00:04:50,682
Most teams running this for the first time find the honest answer is zero.

75
00:04:50,682 --> 00:04:52,759
The data was captured faithfully.

76
00:04:52,759 --> 00:04:55,592
Nobody ever closed the loop on a single case.

77
00:04:55,592 --> 00:04:58,236
That gap is usually not a tooling problem.

78
00:04:58,236 --> 00:05:01,698
It's a missing name next to a recurring calendar entry.

79
00:05:01,698 --> 00:05:04,657
Widen the check if the first five surprise you.

80
00:05:04,657 --> 00:05:06,168
Pull twenty, then fifty.

81
00:05:06,168 --> 00:05:11,141
The pattern that shows up repeatedly across a real sample is usually the single

82
00:05:11,141 --> 00:05:16,429
highest-value fix available to the feature this month, discovered for the cost of an

83
00:05:16,429 --> 00:05:19,765
hour's honest review instead of a quarter's guessing.

84
00:05:20,315 --> 00:05:22,080
One thing before you move on.

85
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:26,158
Run the check from the example slide for real, on your own feature.

86
00:05:26,158 --> 00:05:31,149
It's a five-minute look that usually tells you exactly where your loop is actually

87
00:05:31,149 --> 00:05:31,453
open.

88
00:05:31,453 --> 00:05:36,322
Then assign it: a weekly thirty-minute review, owned by one person, is enough to

89
00:05:36,322 --> 00:05:37,844
keep a real loop turning.

90
00:05:37,844 --> 00:05:42,895
Both of these are true regardless of how sophisticated your instrumentation already

91
00:05:42,895 --> 00:05:43,078
is.

92
00:05:43,078 --> 00:05:47,460
The most advanced event schema in the world produces nothing on its own.

93
00:05:47,460 --> 00:05:51,172
It only ever produces what someone deliberately does with it.

94
00:05:51,172 --> 00:05:55,981
Next lecture is dashboards leadership actually reads, and the honest difference

95
00:05:55,981 --> 00:06:00,971
between a dashboard built to inform a decision and one built to look thorough in a

96
00:06:00,971 --> 00:06:01,397
review.
